WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:05.080
Running AI on your own machine keeps data
off third-party servers and swaps

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:10.580
unpredictable API bills for a fixed upfront cost.
The trade-off is you need the

3
00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:16.880
right hardware. Step one, the single most
important part is the GPU's video memory,

4
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:23.060
or VRAM. If a model fits entirely in VRAM,
you get fast, fluid chat. If it

5
00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:29.540
overflows to system RAM, it slows to a crawl.
Step two, a starter setup with a

6
00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:35.380
12 gigabyte GPU and 32 gigs of RAM runs
7 billion parameter models for around $1,200

7
00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:41.140
to $1,500. A mid-tier build with a 24 gigabyte
card jumps to about $2,500 and

8
00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:48.300
comfortably runs models up to 30 billion
parameters. Step three, the GPU handles

9
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:53.140
the heavy math, but your system RAM and
storage matter too. You'll want at least

10
00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:58.140
32 gigabytes of RAM and a fast SSD with
half a terabyte or more to store

11
00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:05.140
chunky model files. Step four, if your team
spends over $500 a month on API calls,

12
00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:11.220
a mid-tier self-hosting setup typically
pays for itself within six months. After

13
00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:16.300
that, you're mainly paying for electricity.
The best move is to start with a small

14
00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:21.180
quantized 7 billion parameter model on
affordable hardware. Prove it works for

15
00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:25.540
your use case first, then scale up. The tools
are ready and the math favors owning

16
00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:28.060
your own stack.
